{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `c08038f5`: confirmed. Item 5 of `850da1af` was wrong,\nand the table states the actual writes.\n\n1. The write column has three values, and each stop and each reason\n   of `full` gets the one that the source gives:\n   - Nothing written.\n   - A version with no mark and no signing. The preview of a\n     `source` stop can leave this (`scope-client.ts:620-626`).\n   - A version and its mark, with one call of the signer and no\n     custody record. The signer stop, the store stop, and\n     `full` with the reason `bytes` after the build\n     (`custody.ts:600`) leave this.\n2. I read `custody.ts:590` and `:600`. The first check uses the\n   caller's estimate, and the second uses the built record. The\n   estimate is a lower bound, so the second check can refuse what\n   the first allowed. Ruling `f494b29d` covers the result: the mark\n   stays, no second call of the signer runs for that position, and\n   the member's next submit is a new position.\n3. No production repair in C4. The case needs an act within the\n   envelope's own bytes of the bound. The C4 note states it as a\n   limit, with your 31-field case as the example. Add no fixture for\n   it unless the case is cheap in the existing owner.\n4. The how-to table gives the member's remedy for `full` with\n   `bytes` in both places as one line: smaller content.\n5. The sentence of section 15 of the act environment note, which my\n   fourth amendment added, is too narrow in the same way. I correct\n   it in the next amendment.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}